# The Hero Takes His Leave
Tamil title: வீரன் விடை பெற்றுக் கொள்கிறார்

“Long live! Long live! Welcome! Welcome! Our leader, welcome!” The roar of greeting rises to the heavens. His majestic stride inspires thousands upon thousands of hearts.

“What love they bear me! What affection and devotion!” he thinks, seeing the loving enthusiasm in the gathering, and his face blossoms.

There is the fragrance of jasmine, and a gentle smile naturally appears in his gait. A black cobra lies upon the path. Before his eyes he sees only a flower garden, smiling jasmine and mullai; his thoughts settle upon their beauty. The black cobra waits to strike.

Many thousands have gathered to greet and welcome him. Having heard his word, they wait like arrows fitted to bows. He rejoices at their inward delight. “Is not this love the rarest ornament one can obtain? Is it not a fortress that breaks the point of the enemy’s sharp sword? Having gained this, what else remains to be gained?” he thinks, his eyes dancing with joy. The murderer waits to kill.

The veteran of many fields approaches; the encampment welcomes him. A sinner waits to commit a monstrous evil. He comes with a stately stride. Countless warriors welcome him crying, “Our leader comes!” The traitor feels his pistol.

“Welcome, founder of a state! Welcome, man of power! Welcome, fearless heart! Welcome, companion of the poor! Welcome, you who dwell in our hearts!” They offer many such greetings. He resolves inwardly, “I have received their love as an offering; I shall serve them still more.” Elsewhere, a human beast dares to kill him.

Had that villain paused for one moment and thought, “Can I kill this man of rare virtue, beloved by thousands upon thousands, who created a new state for them?” his mind might have recovered. He did not think. He did not know the vileness of his deed. Courage and wisdom stood embodied before him. The people placed garlands of love upon their leader. The criminal fired.

Flower garlands lay upon his chest like ornaments signifying the people’s affection. The great sinner’s bullet struck that chest. The hero fell. The traitor fired. The assembly writhed like a worm cast into fire. Pakistan’s guardian, the treasure discovered by Quaid-e-Azam, Asia’s remarkable man, the leader whose ability won him high regard in the courts of the world, Liaquat Ali Khan, fell upon the platform. The iron grip of death refused to release him. The assembly could not comprehend it. The garland was drenched in blood. The hero took his leave. His task had ended. The sinner had drunk his life. The last page of a heroic volume of history appeared. Liaquat was gone, sacrificed to a great sinner’s bullet.

In ancient days the Frontier Province was an abode of massacres, but those killings arose when sword met sword. This murder was not of that kind. A criminal treacherously killed a man armed with intellect.

He came to offer counsel and explain the manner of government. Had this man come to answer that counsel and oppose that system? No. The cruel man came to murder. A popular assembly gathered to gain knowledge by hearing Pakistan’s Prime Minister explain affairs. The sinner sat in that gathering to shoot him dead.

He came to pluck a flower, the flower of the people’s love. A black cobra lay coiled there to strike him. It struck. The sacred lamp went out. Darkness spread across Pakistan. Shock struck all our hearts. Noble souls throughout the world trembled on hearing of the murder.

Who was this villain? Why did he do it? Such questions arise only after tears have dried. On hearing the news, the first thought is, “Did this fate befall him?” It shocks us.

The public meeting was in Rawalpindi. The man of action who laboured without fatigue to turn Pakistan into a radiant land had come to speak. Another Godse arose, wearing an Islamic form but possessing Godse’s heart. As though wishing to discover what thought inhabited his breast, the crowd reportedly killed the murderer and tore him apart. Yet what use was that? They killed the cobra, but before they could do so, the cobra had killed the man of action.

Here, one man killed the lord who was the embodiment of love, in the evening at a prayer meeting. The whole land spat in the direction of that villain. In Pakistan too such a sinner arose to kill Liaquat, in the evening at a popular assembly.

Immediately after the bullets struck, Pakistan’s guardian was taken to hospital. It was useless. The great hero died. Beside the tomb of the departed Jinnah, a new grave has arisen. Two great heroes, peerless leaders bestowed upon the Islamic world, have passed away. The pilots of a vessel moving through a turbulent sea are gone.

Yes, both were pilots. Their loss is unquestionably so immense that it creates anxiety over whether the vessel itself faces great danger. Yet while they lived, they did more than steer it skilfully. They performed an even more capable task: they formed and left behind a disciplined rank of able people with the skill to steer the vessel. Tears will therefore rise within it, and distress and bewilderment will occur. But those leaders performed their duty and departed. People resolved to continue their work will keep the vessel moving.

As our dear Rajagopalachari has pointed out, Burma’s ministers were murdered, but the Burmese government continued. The noble Gandhi was shot down; others wiped their tears and came forward to perform their duty. So too in Pakistan. This is a death that confounds the mind, an immense loss not easily repaired, but the people of Pakistan must gain consolation and courage and perform their duty.

As neighbours, we share their sorrow. Every nation of the world comes forward to wipe it away. Differences, grievances, rivalry, and malicious thoughts vanished in the shock caused by the news of Liaquat’s brutal murder. They must vanish entirely. Working towards that end will prove that we honour the departed hero.

There lies the grave, the grave of a founder of a nation. The state that Babur and others established by the sword was dissolved by the British. Armed with intellect, Jinnah and Liaquat created the new state called Pakistan. It was first mocked as a foolish notion sketched upon paper; today a new picture named Pakistan has been added to the world’s atlas. A fanatic shot and killed the man successfully engaged in the rare task of filling that new state with splendour and strength. The fool did not consider the consequences.

For Liaquat, this death was honourable. It may be called one more among his countless victories. His was not a death gained after the grip weakened, the gait failed, sight dimmed, position vanished, and a racking cough brought lamentations of continued pain while neighbours wondered why life had not yet left; not a death won after an exhausted struggle with a wavering breath.

Before his eyes stood a vast multitude offering tribute. In his ears rang “Long live! Long live!” Upon the platform waited deputy leaders ready to hear his command and enter the field. Death came in that condition. Even while caught in its grasp, in the final second, he had the opportunity to serve the people. He did not confront the heartbreak of having to abandon principle, see plans reduced to dust, or watch influence decay. He saw victory, more victory, continuous victory, and at the end a warrior’s death. His history has therefore become a textbook prepared by this generation for the heroic martyrs of the future.

Yet this murder raises another problem demanding thought.

When Gandhi was killed by an assassin, this country and the world asked: can such savagery appear in public life? If human beasts are allowed to roam, how can dignity survive? Why do people bereft of reason introduce the ways of the jungle into a nation? How does society permit these vile men? Such questions arose and gnawed at the heart.

You dislike a policy? Very well. Present another policy to the people that will send the first to the rubbish heap, and win. That requires intelligence and ability; it also requires humanity and culture. Only then will the country avoid becoming a jungle. You dislike a party? Very well. Strengthen your own party until the people will not even look at the other. That requires skill and abundant clarity. Only then will politics become the dwelling place of civilisation.

Under a savage king, one of the people suffering beneath his injustice might think that eliminating the sinner would reform the country and save good people, and commit an assassination. It might even serve as a remedy.

But we live in democratic times. We proudly tell the world that we possess the ability to operate that exalted political machine. If we truly believe in that ability, why should dislike of a party, policy, programme, or law produce hatred of those responsible, irritation, and the wolfish urge to obliterate them beyond recognition? The proper course is to explain to the people the conduct of a disliked party and the unsuitability of its policies so that they understand.

Regrettably, this quality appears only here and there, now and then, like a stream in a desert. In general it is deficient, and we must say with alarm that it is declining. If public life in a democracy falls into the hands of people without culture, murder becomes the accepted method. What use is general advice inviting people to drink tender coconut water after releasing wolves to wander among them?

Clashing with individuals is neither politics nor public service. It is street-corner violence, the way of the jungle. Unless this tendency is uprooted from democracy, which rests upon the exalted principle that everyone has a right to govern, we will not deserve the dignity of popular rule. We might as well make skulls and garlands of bone the emblems on party flags, call the man who stabs and tears out entrails a volunteer, and the man who swings his weapon and severs heads a leader, turn the country into a forest, and erect seats of government inside caves.

Political assassination is not merely unjust; it is a mark of barbarism. The reward of possessing eyes is to witness the leader of an opposing party realise that his policy was wrong, kneel before you, and say, “Until today I spoke senselessly, but the light of knowledge I found in your argument revealed the truth, and I now accept your policy.” Is it human conduct instead to hand a sickle to a hireling, intoxicate and incite him, and watch him roll an opponent’s head to the ground? Are these not the deeds of tigers, wolves, and other forest creatures: growling, leaping, biting, tearing, killing, and drinking blood? These are the ways of the animal world.

Whenever human beasts adopt them, we should understand that the jungle has not been wholly removed from the nation. Rolling one’s eyes at an opponent, pouring out abuse, threatening with a roar, delighting in stone throwing, and brandishing a knife with a triumphant cry repeatedly appear in public life. Good people withdraw saying, “Why involve ourselves?” and people of virtue wring their hands and move away. Thus a disposition as cruel as Godse’s grows.

Political assassinations do not occur in lands where civilisation, culture, and faith in reason flourish. Here we have seen two great murders, murders that split the heart. Unless we strive to change the conditions that nourish such degradation, the world will soon declare us unfit for democracy. The method of gathering to speak and working together will disappear, and savage rule will bind us tightly. Liaquat’s assassination places this common problem before every mind’s eye.

Culture must grow if democracy is to succeed. We must admit that even the measure of this quality found among ordinary people is absent among some who organise and run parties for public service.

A thief enters a house. On one side he sees a mother and small children asleep, with jewels glittering. On the other he sees one man sleeping. At once the thief thinks that if the man awakens, he must lift a large stone and kill him, and he may do it. But if he sees four or five men lying there, the thought of murder will not arise; he will think only of slipping quietly away. Political rivalry follows the same rule.

Suppose all responsibility for a party or a national administration is concentrated in one person: framing plans, explaining them and gathering support, shaping that support, and acting through the organisation thus formed. Suppose further that no one else possesses this ability. A human beast rejoices: “Ah! Is this one man not making everything dance? Finish him, and it is all finished; the country will be in our hands.” Political assassination then becomes possible.

But if party administration or national responsibility combines the talents of many, and if one can point to many capable people, an entire trained and skilled rank of workers, then even a human beast may think: “What use is killing the individual who stands first in the front line? If he goes, the next will come; if that person too goes, a third stands ready. Murder will achieve nothing.”

Therefore, to prevent political assassination in democracies, culture must develop, and parties and governing groups must create a trained rank of workers. If a leader is murdered by a sinner, there must never be no one else able to act in the field. Many must be ready to proclaim, “The hero has fallen. Let us salute him and continue the work where he left it.” The number and ability of public workers must grow until even the wicked understand that assassination yields no benefit.

Liaquat is gone. It is a great loss. But what can be done? Many remain to say, “We shall work to the extent of our ability,” and perform their duty. May their ability serve to end hostility, raise culture, let peace flourish, and establish reconciliation.

Liaquat shed sweat to establish Pakistan; now he has shed his blood too. May that blood help Pakistan gain strength, and not strength alone but culture; and beyond that, may it help Pakistan cultivate relations with neighbouring countries and perform the noble work of leaving no opening in Asia for the vultures of imperialism.

We offer a warrior’s salute to the departed hero. We convey our profound sympathy to Pakistan, reeling under indescribable anguish, and to Liaquat’s family.

## Biographical Notes

Janab Liaquat Ali Khan was born in 1895 in Karnal district of Punjab.

After completing his studies in India, he went to England, studied at Oxford University, and received a degree.

In 1921 he first joined the Muslim League and took an active part in the politics of the United Provinces.

In 1937 he became General Secretary of the Muslim League.

In 1946 he became Finance Minister in India’s Interim Government and implemented notable economic policies.

After Partition in 1947, he went to Pakistan and served as its Prime Minister.

During his life, he asked for a beautiful nation in which his community might live, and he obtained it. World history contains very few great heroes who fulfilled within their own lifetimes the ideal they conceived. His victorious life shows Liaquat Ali Khan to be one among them.

(Published in *Dravida Nadu*, 21 October 1951.)

---NOTES---
“வீரன் விடை பெற்றுக் கொள்கிறார்” literally presents death as the hero taking leave; the translation retains that memorial idiom.
“காயிதே அஃலம்” is the source’s Tamil rendering of *Quaid-e-Azam*, the honorific associated with Muhammad Ali Jinnah.
The essay repeatedly compares Liaquat Ali Khan’s assassination with Gandhi’s and uses the comparison to argue for democratic political culture and distributed institutional leadership.
“சந்துமுனை நடவடிக்கை” denotes street-corner or factional violence rather than reasoned politics.
The OCR rendered the publication date as `21-19-50`; scan `014-fc813bd4e5.png` clearly reads `21-10-51`, which has been restored in the canonical Tamil source.
